    "summary": "The mosaic button with its field stood on its corner: the same two plates, the same solid rim on the inner one's edge, the same fade running in from the top edge and in from the bottom, and that checker on a 5px pitch, turned 45 degrees.",
    "description": "The mosaic button with its field stood on its corner: the same two plates, the same solid rim on the inner one's edge, the same fade running in from the top edge and in from the bottom, and that checker on a 5px pitch, turned 45 degrees. The turn is on the layer rather than in the gradient, because a background image cannot be rotated and the field cannot be authored at the angle either. The usual way of drawing a checker on the diagonal, two 45 degree gradients inking each tile's opposite corner triangles, only looks like one: neighbouring triangles fuse across the tile edges straight back into the squares of an upright checker, and the pattern comes out identical to the one it was meant to turn. So the element carrying the field is what spins, and only it, since the fades are its siblings and stay level. A rotated square has to be wider than the box it covers, so that element is a square 200 percent of the plate's width, centred on it and turned, its inscribed circle then reaching every corner of anything wider than it is tall, with the plate's own overflow clip cutting the overhang back to the rim. The cells keep their 50 percent coverage, so the field is held the same distance back. Everything else is the mosaic button's: the outer plate is the real box and carries only a fill, the inner one is the same shape inset by 2px so the label sits where the matte button's label sits, hover brightens the plate and screens a blurred sheet onto the lower half, and the pitch holds through every size so the chip grows around the field rather than scaling it. What is new is what it says. This is the set's face for anything a model does, so it opens on a filled black hole glyph and a Generate label, and the sizes carry a rewrite, an inference run and an assistant instead of four ways of saying buy.",
