    "title": "Strata Button",
    "summary": "The prism button's chassis ruled instead of tiled: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, a 1px line every 3px across the face, and a fade running in from the top edge and in from the bottom over the top of it.",
    "description": "The prism button's chassis ruled instead of tiled: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, a 1px line every 3px across the face, and a fade running in from the top edge and in from the bottom over the top of it. The lines are one repeating gradient rather than a tiled image, drawn in the label's own ink, so there is no asset to load and the plate, the rim and the hatch all read out of the theme. They sit a little forward of the prism's checker, since a line every 3px inks a third of the face where that checker inks a half. The layer carrying them is turned 45 degrees, and turning is what makes a rule worth having on a chip this size: level lines run parallel to the rim and read as a border that got away, where the same lines on the diagonal cross the face and read as a hatch. That turn is the prism's own mechanism, since a background image cannot be rotated: a square 200 percent of the plate's width is centred on it and spun, its inscribed circle reaching every corner of anything wider than it is tall, with the plate's overflow clip cutting the overhang back to the rim. Everything else is the prism's: the outer plate is the real box and carries only a fill, the inner one is the same shape inset by 2px so the label sits where the matte button's label sits, hover brightens the plate and screens a blurred sheet onto the lower half, and the spacing holds through every size so the chip grows around the field rather than scaling it. What is its own is what it says. It opens on a filled shopping bag over a Shop now label, since a mark and a label that name two different jobs read as two buttons stacked on one chip. The preview row is what the same face does elsewhere: a release push, a merge, a pipeline run and a deploy, each on its own filled glyph.",
