    "name": "light-stair",
    "title": "Stair Field",
    "summary": "The ground under Wavefront, on its own with nothing standing on it.",
    "description": "The ground under Wavefront, on its own with nothing standing on it. The light walks the grid rather than falling down it: a streak runs one line end to end, which is a straight shot however it is timed, where a stair turns, up one cell, across one cell, up again, so every corner it makes is a corner of a cell and the whole thing is a route through the grid rather than a slide over it. Every vertex lands on an intersection by construction rather than by measurement, because the plane's top-left corner is one and a path laid out in whole pitches from there cannot be anywhere else. Eight of them stand in pairs, one foot on each side of the middle, and each climbs towards the other, so the light sets off from a bottom corner, works its way in and up, and is gone at the horizon; a pair shares a clock, since the two sides of a scan are one event, and each pair after it is a beat behind. The stair is drawn as a mask and lit by a plain band travelling up behind it, which is the same trick the columns are lit by: the mask holds still, so what climbs is the plane lighting up step by step rather than a shape sliding along a line, and because the band crosses a whole tread at one height a step lights all at once, which is what keeps the run reading as stepped. Four treads are lit at a time, because a single tread this far back is a dash and it is the run of them turning corners that says the light is following the grid. The rain is off: a stair is already an answer to where the light goes, and streaks falling past it would be a second one. The streak's palette is a night-into-fire ramp, deep navy through a teal shoulder to a sand and amber core and out through burnt orange, carried at zero alpha at both black ends because the light layer is additive on dark, where black already contributes nothing, but multiplied on light, where an opaque black end would draw a dark bar down the grid instead of a light leaving it. The grid stays `--border`, so the field is the host's own ink and the streaks are the one thing carrying a colour. Eyebrow, heading and note are props, so the plane can carry someone else's words.",
